--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -1,3 +1,23 @@
 # GPUIX
 
+### Optional native device preview (Neoism overlay)
+
+Build with `--no-default-features --features device-preview` to register `<device_viewport viewportId={id}>`. Linux uses GPUI’s own wgpu device/queue and direct texture compositor: indexed original triangle meshes, a depth buffer, 4× MSAA, and procedural studio environment reflections. There is no browser, normal-frame readback, second device, raymarch, or animation timer. The longest render edge is capped at 1536 pixels to preserve HiDPI edges. Full-window anchored portals without an explicit fill remain transparent; the popup card itself supplies its opaque fill. FreeBSD shares a compile-gated GPUI path but remains unvalidated; Neoism advertises Linux only.
+
+`renderer.createDeviceViewport(optionsJson?: string): number`, `updateDeviceViewport(id, optionsJson): void`, `putDeviceFrame(id, width, height, rgba: Buffer): void`, `clearDeviceFrame(id): void`, and `dropDeviceViewport(id): void` retain bounded latest-frame RGBA8 uploads (4096×4096 / 64 MiB, 16 live viewports). Increment the host’s `frameRevision` after updates/uploads/clears to request painting. Pose-only changes never reupload screen pixels; same-sized video frames reuse screen bindings. Device loss rebuilds resources from the recovered GPUI context.
+
+Options merge as `{model, color, keyboard, display, orientation, yaw, pitch, hinge, zoom, flat}`. `model` is exactly `iphone-18-pro` (default), `iphone-18-pro-max`, `iphone-duo`, or `ipad-pro-13-m5`. `color` is three normalized sRGB numbers. `keyboard` rotates the complete tablet/camera/glass geometry into landscape above a wide keyboard/stand/keys/trackpad base, with upright landscape source UVs; keyboard-off remains portrait. `display` is `inner` (default) or `cover` (Duo only); hinge is Duo-only radians from open/flat, clamped to 0..π. A zero-degree opening is exactly π fold, with a 0.02-unit inner-glass air gap from the hinge clearance offsets; there is no hidden angular clamp. Default yaw/pitch are 0/0, a dead-front rest pose; native bounds framing targets approximately 88.5% of the limiting viewport dimension. Original proportional designs include rounded bevel bodies/glass, occluding camera cutouts, Pro triple lens platforms/rings/glass, tablet camera, side buttons/seams/ports/speaker perforations, and an articulated Duo hinge with combined inner and separate cover screen. They are not official CAD and use no copied models or textures. `flat` contain-fits the raw frame without a device. Live video uses an exact unlit sRGB texture path with black contain-fit letterboxing: no relighting, reflections, gamma conversion or tone mapping is applied to the frame. `orientation` accepts portrait (default), portrait_upside_down, landscape_left and landscape_right from confirmed host metadata. Bare conventional models rotate the complete mesh and remap source UVs upright; orientation participates in the mesh cache key, not per-frame uploads. Keyboard and Duo ignore generic orientation and should keep Rotate disabled until their host-specific protocol is wired.
+
+`hitDeviceViewport(id, x, y, width, height): string` accepts local layout pixels and returns JSON `null` or `{x,y,display}` with normalized source-frame coordinates. It intersects the same nearest visible triangles used by the rasterizer, including hinge/accessory geometry and camera cutout occlusion, then applies each screen’s native UV/aspect/contain mapping. Bezel, backs, keys, bars, outside coordinates and missing frames return null. `facingDeviceViewport(id): string` reports `inner` or `cover` using visible screen-triangle probes; it is intended for Duo display selection, not a second JS camera model. GPU rendering and CPU picks share camera transforms and logical viewport aspect even when the bounded render target rounds pixel dimensions.
+
+Headless native `device_viewport` tests render all profiles/front/back, folded cover and tablet keyboard to `artifacts/device-preview/lineup-front-back.png` and individual PNGs plus `accessories-contact-sheet.png`. Keyboard evidence uses an original neutral landscape wave frame (not a live OS capture); the cover proof is rendered at exactly π fold. Viewport output texture COPY_SRC usage is test-only. Normal rendering performs no readback; explicit window-capture requests use the separate on-demand scene API described below.
+
+Materials use linear-space GGX distribution, height-correlated Smith visibility and Schlick Fresnel, with exact sRGB EOTF/OETF and no global Reinhard tone mapping. Original analytic softboxes approximate rough room reflections without borrowed environment assets. Conventional devices use a 32° vertical FOV and the reference ambient/key/rim/fill values; Duo uses 36° and its room-style key/fill. Vertex/index buffers, screen textures and bindings remain cached; framing is recalculated only for pose/aspect changes. Rear camera shelves, inset lower glass, rounded rails, convex optical caps/coatings and keyboard geometric legends are authored here, based on inspected silhouettes and scalar material/bounds metadata, not copied mesh or texture payloads.
+
+`preview: boolean` (default false) displays original static abstract artwork only when no frame is present; callers must enable it only for an explicitly offline model preview, never to conceal a failed stream. Duo supports both physical presentations through `duoBook: boolean` (default false). False preserves the portrait top/bottom clamshell, with a final rig quarter turn about Z; true retains the broad side-by-side book rig without that geometry turn. Raw portrait inner frames quarter-turn into the book surface exactly once; already-landscape frames avoid double rotation. Both paths contain-fit, and picks return coordinates in the actual uploaded frame. Closed portrait cover pose is yaw 0, pitch -π/2, hinge π; closed book cover is yaw π/2, pitch 0, hinge π. Framing, facing, and picking share the transformed mesh. Changing duoBook rebakes only Duo geometry; preview/capture/frame-upload generations and unrelated profiles are unchanged. Headless proofs include duo-book-raw-portrait.png, duo-book-landscape.png, duo-book-offline-preview.png and duo-book-closed-cover.png.
+
+Optional automation bounds queries read the last published painted map rather than synchronously waiting for a future UI frame. With device-preview on Linux, renderer.captureScreenshot(path) sends an explicit debug command to the UI thread and calls Window::render_to_image() on its last completed scene, including custom device textures, then saves PNG and responds without on_next_frame, another present, or another device. The independently pinned optional GPUI window-capture overlay supplies the Wayland/X11 same-renderer readback implementation and bounds it to 4096 pixels per axis. No viewport path or idle frame performs readback. Native headless PNG fixtures are model-only evidence; actual full-panel capture must be exercised on the optimized running app separately and is not claimed from the unit fixture tests.
+
+Before/after evidence is under artifacts/device-preview: before/lineup-front-back.png is the preserved prior renderer; after/matched-camera-lineup.png uses exactly its fixed 32° camera, pose, finish inputs and original colorful wallpaper, with before-after-contact-sheet.png placing them top/bottom. The current lineup-front-back.png instead uses production bounds fitting, reference-like finishes and a separate original neutral wave wallpaper at each source aspect. The two fixture categories are intentionally distinguished.
+
 `GpuixRenderer.initAsync(options)` returns a promise for native window readiness.
